<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- Entitlements applied to the direct-download build by the codesign re-sign in
     build-dmg.sh. This is NOT the target's CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS — that stays
     empty in VniDropDirect.entitlements.

     Why the split: the Rust core keeps secrets in the data-protection Keychain
     (kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain, see secure_secret/apple.rs). That keychain
     only answers to a process resolving to a keychain access group, so without
     one every SecItem call returns errSecMissingEntitlement (-34018), which the
     core surfaces as VnidropError::SecureStorageUnavailable and the app turns
     into a startup failure. That is the 0.3.1 bug: the direct build shipped an
     empty entitlements dict, so no secret could ever be written or read.

     The group cannot simply be declared on the target, because naming
     keychain-access-groups in CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS makes `xcodebuild archive`
     fail with "VniDropDirect requires a provisioning profile", and Developer ID
     distribution deliberately has no profile. Plain codesign enforces no such
     requirement, and the re-sign is what produces the signature that actually
     ships — so the group is applied there instead.

     $(DEVELOPMENT_TEAM) and $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER) are NOT expanded by
     codesign; build-dmg.sh resolves them with sed and fails the build if any
     placeholder survives. -->
<dict>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_TEAM).$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
